1
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:06,900
Campus teams have limited repair budgets.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,900
In 2022, about one in eight U.S. adults reported
serious difficulty walking or climbing stairs.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,700
At Princeton’s Robertson Hall,
we filmed a real sidewalk with a phone.

4
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:26,100
Clicking Finding Four takes us
to the broken surface at six seconds.

5
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:30,100
The five findings were prepared with AI.

6
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:37,900
A reviewer can check the footage
and flag what still needs a site measurement.

7
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,900
For the budget test, we switch
to a separate simulated campus.

8
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,900
Give both plans ten thousand dollars.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,500
Worst condition first funds repair C
and opens one destination, Hall.

10
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:59,300
Curb picks two linked repairs, A and B,
and opens the Library and Student center.

11
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:04,900
Same budget, two newly reachable destinations
versus one in this example.

12
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,700
Take A away and both new trips disappear.

13
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:16,100
Put A back and take B away.
The same thing happens.

14
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,900
The shared route needs both repairs.

15
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:27,200
At twenty thousand dollars,
both plans can fund all three repairs.

16
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:31,900
They tie at three newly reachable destinations.

17
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:39,900
The decision brief keeps the recommended work
and the model’s assumptions together.

18
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:43,050
The buyer is campus facilities.

19
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:51,900
Next, we’d walk the crossings with disabled campus users
and accessibility staff, measure them, and get quotes.
